# Reference output for the headless renderer.
#
# `--headless` runs on a fixed timestep with no terminal control, so a fixed
# seed produces the same bytes every time. Checking those bytes against a
# committed hash is what turns that property into a test with an opinion: the
# run-to-run comparison beside it only says a flight is repeatable, and a
# change that quietly alters every frame is repeatable too.
#
# Checked two ways, from one file. `cargo test --test golden` reproduces the
# bytes in process, so a renderer that has drifted fails on the machine that
# drifted it; the `headless` CI job produces the same files from a release
# binary and runs `sha256sum -c` over this, which also proves the two agree.
#
# Seven flights, sharing
#
#     --headless --frames 120 --seed 1 --size 120x36
#
# and differing in what they ask the renderer to draw:
#
#     truecolor.txt   --demo --color truecolor
#     ascii.txt       --demo --color ascii
#     ansi256.txt     --demo --color 256
#     warp.txt        --engage --throttle 1.0 --color truecolor
#     side.txt        --engage --throttle 1.0 --view side --color truecolor
#     orbit.txt       the same, --orbit 55,35,20
#     astern.txt      the same, --orbit -75,6,20
#
# The last one is the camera swung off the beam, and it is here for the reason
# the two before it are: the outside view's whole geometry is written to reduce
# *exactly* to the old arithmetic when the camera is abeam, which is where
# `side.txt` has it. So a change that repainted every angle except that one
# would have left the reference untouched. `--orbit 55,35,20` has all three
# angles off zero at once, which is the only configuration that turns the
# bubble's outline off the horizontal, and it puts the star band's depth travel,
# its vertical fold and its wall recycle on the path as well.
#
# `astern.txt` is the newest and it is here because the six above it all watch
# the ship from abeam or from in front of it. The camera goes all the way round;
# the point the ship's own *track* vanishes at is on the screen from behind and
# from nowhere else. So half the range of a control had no reference at all, and
# it was the half where a trail stretched in screen space can be drawn clean
# through a place the track only ever approaches. It is recorded at
# `--orbit -75,6,20` — all three angles off zero, the nose receding, and the
# point landing 24 subpixels from the ship and 20 from the nearest edge, which
# are the two conditions that make the question askable at all and are the same
# pair `models::forward_quarter()` picks its angles for.
#
# The three before it are here because the first two turned out to cover very
# little.
# `--demo` spends its opening six seconds easing the throttle up, and 120 frames
# at 60 fps is two of them — so the pinned sky was sublight throughout, peaking
# at a quarter of light speed with the drive cold. Every warp path in the
# renderer sat outside it: the streak ramp, the tunnel glare, the flash, the
# Doppler shift, and the whole view from outside with it — the star band, the
# lens, the arcs and the hulls. A change to any of those could repaint the sky
# and this file would not have noticed.
#
# What the five still do not cover, and it took a fix to find out: the fleet is
# one ship deep. Neither of the two flights with a ship in it passes `--ship`,
# so both fly the enterprise. Clearing the hull band over the span it is read
# over rather than the span it is written to changed real frames for the dart,
# the needle, the hauler and the trident — three of five camera angles for two
# of them — and left every hash below bit-identical, because the enterprise's
# projected outline does not happen to expose it at any angle tried. So a
# change to `Canvas::fill_hull` or `models::plates` that this file passes is
# not thereby a change that draws every hull the way it drew it before, and the
# guard for that one is the property test in `canvas.rs`, not this.
#
# `ansi256.txt` is `truecolor.txt`'s flight in the other colour mode, and it is
# the one case here recorded to be compared against another rather than against
# itself: the two ask for the same sky and differ only in how a cell's colour is
# spelled, so a change that moves one and not the other has landed in the writer
# and not in the renderer. It arrived with the fix that made the mode send
# `38;5;N` — before that it sent palette values inside a 24-bit sequence, which
# the terminals the mode exists for cannot read — and it is recorded because the
# mode had no reference at all while being the one `ColorMode::detect` hands to
# any terminal with a `TERM` entry and no `COLORTERM`, which is most of them.
#
# Regenerate deliberately, with the diff in hand to say what moved:
#
#     cargo build --release
#     common="--headless --frames 120 --seed 1 --size 120x36"
#     ./target/release/warp $common --demo --color truecolor > truecolor.txt
#     ./target/release/warp $common --demo --color ascii     > ascii.txt
#     ./target/release/warp $common --demo --color 256       > ansi256.txt
#     ./target/release/warp $common --engage --throttle 1.0 --color truecolor > warp.txt
#     ./target/release/warp $common --engage --throttle 1.0 --view side --color truecolor > side.txt
#     ./target/release/warp $common --engage --throttle 1.0 --view side --orbit 55,35,20 --color truecolor > orbit.txt
#     ./target/release/warp $common --engage --throttle 1.0 --view side --orbit -75,6,20 --color truecolor > astern.txt
#     sha256sum truecolor.txt ascii.txt ansi256.txt warp.txt side.txt orbit.txt astern.txt \
#         > tests/golden/frames.sha256
#     # and put this comment block back
#
# All seven moved together when the star count stopped following the terminal.
# `--stars` is a plain count now, defaulting to 256, where it used to default to
# 0 for "suit the pool to the canvas" — stars per subpixel, 0.02 where it
# finished. Not one of these seven passes the flag, so every one of them flew on
# that density, and at 120x36 — 8 640 subpixels — that is 172 stars becoming
# 256.
#
# Seven hashes, and this is the shape where the split between them carries
# nothing whatever. Two things move in every frame of every flight for it, the
# sky and the panel's STARS readout that names the number, so a hash standing
# still would have been the thing wanting an explanation — it would mean some
# flight was not sizing its pool from the default at all. This is the third time
# this file has recorded that shape for the star count and it is the last: both
# of the entries below it are the same constant being moved, and there is no
# longer a constant there for a terminal to reach.
#
# `orbit.txt` and `astern.txt` moved together when the band stopped letting the
# sky fall in on itself off the beam. A star's place in the frame is
# `focal · pos / z`, so every change of range scales it, and the fold — which is
# the right answer for the *translation* the flow applies — is no answer at all
# for a scale.
# Contracting, the band widened away from a pool shrinking inside it and nothing
# refilled the frame edges; expanding, the fold put a star back on the very edge
# it had just been carried over and the next step carried it over again, so the
# pool piled into the off-screen margin and the middle drained. Two seconds on
# `A` or `D` was enough to empty the top and bottom of the frame and leave a bar
# of stars across the middle. The step now puts back `1 − λ²` of each range
# shell on the rim a contraction vacates, and scatters what an expansion carried
# out across the whole band rather than wrapping it, both of which are the
# density it started with exactly rather than an approximation of it. The depth
# recycle changed with it: a star crossing a wall now comes back just inside the
# wall the flow is entering through instead of anywhere in the band, which is
# the fold in `z` in distribution and holds the count per unit range flat where
# seeding through the whole band had turned a one-way flow into a seven-to-one
# ramp.
#
# Two hashes, and they are the two flights that have a `travel[2]` at all — a
# sharper split than the entry below, which is the same pair of camera angles
# read the other way round. Every new branch is gated on `turns_z`, which is
# `swing.is_some() || travel[2] != 0.0`: at `Orbit::LEVEL` travel is exactly
# `(-1, 0, 0)` and `--orbit` is snapped at startup, so nothing in a headless
# `side.txt` step reaches any of it and the arithmetic is the old arithmetic to
# the bit. The three cockpit flights and `warp.txt` never build an
# `ExteriorField` at all. `orbit.txt` is at `--orbit 55,35,20`, where
# `travel[2]` is `cos 35° · sin 55° = 0.671`, and `astern.txt` at
# `--orbit -75,6,20`, where it is `cos 6° · sin −75° = −0.961` — one flight
# contracting the sky and one expanding it, which is the pair of failure modes
# the change is about. Both move for three reasons at once: the recycle draws
# from a different range so the RNG stream diverges, the range profile goes from
# a ramp to flat, and the transverse boundary is now maintained.
#
# Note what that means for this file again. Both were recorded *with* the
# collapse in them, and neither could have caught it in any case, because both
# are shot at `--throttle 1.0` where one step is longer than the whole
# three-hundred-deep band and a star is recycled before it can drift anywhere.
# It shows at ten c and not at two thousand — so a seventh flight was added for
# a fault it could not see either, one commit before this one. The guard is
# `the_sky_is_as_even_off_the_beam_as_it_is_abeam` in `exterior.rs`, which
# measures the pool's spread across the frame rather than a hash of it, at the
# speed the report was shot at.
#
# `astern.txt` arrived with the fix for a fault not one of the six could see,
# and the useful half of that entry is that **no hash moved for it**. A trail is
# stretched back along the track in *screen* space — the step the star swept,
# multiplied by six at full warp — and a ray running away from the eye does not
# project onto a screen-space ray. It projects onto the segment between the star
# and the point its own direction vanishes at, approaching it and never
# arriving, so the multiply drew trail past the place it had already gone. It is
# `models::draw_trail`'s bug, in the star band, and it wears the same fix:
# `Camera::vanishing_point`, and one division.
#
# Every one of the six answers `None` to that division and so cannot move.
# Abeam the nose lies flat in the image plane; forward of the beam the track
# runs toward the eye and its projection diverges rather than converging; and
# the three cockpit flights are a different module. That is not a change
# vindicated by six green hashes, it is a change that found the hole those six
# leave — and the hole is a whole half of where the camera can be put. Hence
# this flight, and hence `a_trail_never_runs_past_the_point_the_track_vanishes_at`
# in `exterior.rs`, which is the guard at the level the fault lives at: it holds
# every trail in the pool short of that point and no shorter than the step it
# was stretched from. Recorded *after* the fix, so what it pins is the sky with
# the cusp gone.
#
# `orbit.txt` moved on its own when a folded trail stopped being scaled by the
# wrong range. The band wraps a star that runs off an edge round to the other
# side and carries its trail with it, which means turning a world-space jump
# into a screen-space one — a divide by the star's range. It was dividing by the
# range the star *ends* the step at, where the trail had been projected through
# the range it *started* it at, so the trail landed short or long by
# `shift · focal · (1/z_new − 1/z_old)`, along the camera's own x for the
# sideways fold and y for the vertical one. `streaks` then multiplies the whole
# tail-to-head vector by six at full warp, and what reached the frame was a
# lattice of long faint streaks lying across the flow instead of running with
# it — faint because `draw_streak` divides a streak's light by its length, so a
# streak several times longer than the motion it stands for is spread that much
# thinner.
#
# One hash, and it is the only shape this change can have. Travel is exactly
# `(-1, 0, 0)` at `Orbit::LEVEL`, so `travel[2]` is a hard zero, the two ranges
# are the same bit for bit, and the correction was already exact where
# `side.txt` has the camera. The three cockpit flights are a different module
# again. Note which way round that leaves this file: `orbit.txt` was recorded
# with the fault in it and pinned it for as long as it stood, which is what a
# reference frame does with anything nobody has looked at — it was found by
# looking at the README's own screenshot, not by anything here. The guard is
# therefore a property test at the level the fault lives at,
# `a_trail_carried_over_the_fold_is_scaled_by_the_range_it_was_drawn_at` in
# `exterior.rs`, which holds every star's trail to the projection of where it
# was one step back along the track — folded, recycled or merely flown — at
# four camera angles and two speeds.
#
# `side.txt` and `orbit.txt` moved together when three measurements in the
# outside view stopped being taken the expensive way. `hypot` is libm's
# overflow-safe, correctly-rounded routine and every span it was measuring here
# is a difference of two canvas coordinates, so it was buying a guarantee the
# inputs could not need; `Lens::offsets` ended on a divide per axis by two
# numbers fixed for the whole frame; and `draw_path` measured a segment's length
# three more times after already having it. All three are the same arithmetic to
# within a rounding, and none of them is the same arithmetic to the bit.
#
# Two hashes, and it is the shape this change has to have. Nothing in the
# cockpit reaches `lens`, `exterior` or `draw_path` — the tunnel is streaks and
# two glows — so the three `--demo` flights and `warp.txt` came back
# byte-identical, and a hash moving outside these two would have meant the reach
# was wider than the change claimed. It is the same split the hull changes below
# have, arrived at from the other side: those two are the flights with a ship in
# them, and they are also the only two that light a lens.
#
# Those two changes met in a merge, and the hashes below are what the merged
# tree actually renders rather than one side's set kept and the other's
# discarded. Both moved what is drawn, so neither branch's file was right for
# the tree that has both: the density decides how many stars every one of these
# six flights spawns, and the measurements decide where the two that light a
# lens put them. Regenerated from the merge and read against `main` rather than
# against the branch, which is the sharper baseline — `main` already carries the
# density, so `side.txt` and `orbit.txt` differ from it and the other four are
# byte-identical to it, exactly as the paragraph above says they must be.
#
# All six moved together when `AUTO_DENSITY` went from 0.005 to 0.02, and this
# is the second time this file has recorded that shape for that constant — see
# the halving below, which is the change this one partly walks back. The reading
# is the same and it is inverted from the usual one: none of the six passes
# `--stars`, so every one flies on the automatic count, and at 120x36 — 8 640
# subpixels — that took the pool from 43 stars to 172. Two things move in every
# frame for it, the sky and the panel's STARS readout that names the number, so
# a hash standing still would have been the thing wanting an explanation. The
# split between the six carries no information about this change at all; what it
# would carry is news that some flight was not sizing its pool from the density.
#
# `orbit.txt` moved on its own when the drive stopped shining through the hull.
# The bells and the exhaust they throw are light, the plates are opaque, and
# `models::draw` laid the first over the second unconditionally — so from
# anywhere forward of the beam the enterprise's nacelle bells burned as two blue
# lamps in the middle of a saucer that was standing squarely in front of them.
# The drive is now drawn on whichever side of the plates it belongs on, which is
# a question about where the exhaust is pointed rather than about the camera.
#
# One hash and only one, and that is the shape this change has to have. The
# three cockpit flights draw no hull and no bells at all. `side.txt` is the
# abeam shot, where the ship's axis lies flat in the image plane and the nozzles
# point across the eye rather than away from it: the depth the axis gains over a
# unit of its length is a hard zero there — the standoff is on both sides of the
# subtraction and cancels bit for bit — so the drive is drawn after the plates
# exactly as it always was, in one call with its share at exactly 1.0. Only the
# flight with the camera swung round to 55 degrees of azimuth is looking at a
# ship whose drive it should not be able to see through.
#
# All five moved together when the automatic star count was halved and its floor
# taken away, and this is the one change where the split between these hashes
# carries no information at all. None of the five passes `--stars`, so every one
# of them flies on the automatic count, and at 120x36 — 8 640 subpixels — that
# went from the old floor of 300 stars to 43. Two things changed in every frame
# for it: the sky, and the panel's STARS readout that names the number. So the
# usual reading is inverted here — a hash that had *not* moved would have been
# the thing needing an explanation, since it would mean a flight was not sizing
# its pool from the density at all.
#
# Before that, `side.txt` and `orbit.txt` moved when the engine lance was given
# a tail that fades to nothing, and was aimed at its vanishing point instead of
# being stopped short of it. The lance used to give up 8% of the way out, and
# that margin was never about the geometry: `draw_streak` ramps down to
# `TAIL_BRIGHTNESS` rather than to nothing, and every bell on a ship shares one
# vanishing point, so a lance ending *on* it left a third of full brightness
# from every lane of every bell on a single subpixel — a bead hanging in the sky
# exactly where the exhaust was meant to have gone. `draw_fading_streak` takes
# the floor away, the sample that lands on the point carries a ramp of exactly
# zero, and the margin had nothing left to buy.
#
# Two hashes and not one, which is a different shape from the clamp that went in
# before it and the right one. That change only altered what happens off the
# beam, so it could not reach `side.txt`; this one changes the ramp everywhere a
# drive burns, and both of these flights have the drive lit. The three cockpit
# flights draw no hull and so no plume, and they came back bit-for-bit — which
# is also what says the star path is untouched, since the ramp there is the same
# multiply and the same add on the same constants it always was.
#
# Before that, `orbit.txt` moved on its own when the lance stopped running
# *through* the point its plume vanishes at. A lit drive's lance is stretched in
# screen space to the frame diagonal, and a plume running away from the eye
# projects onto a point that only ever *approaches* the vanishing point of its
# own direction — so the stretch drew exhaust past the place it had already
# gone, and a symmetric pair of bells swapped sides on the way through and
# crossed. `side.txt` could not move for it: it is the abeam shot, where
# `Eye::to_camera` is exactly `(x, y, z) -> (z, y, distance - x)`, the hull's
# axis lies flat in the image plane, its depth term is exactly zero, there is no
# vanishing point to clamp against, and the arithmetic was the old arithmetic to
# the bit.
#
# Before that again, `side.txt` and `orbit.txt` moved together when the hull's
# outline started being measured on a grid inside each subpixel rather than at
# its centre. Those two
# are the flights with a ship in them and the only two that moved for it: the
# three cockpit flights came back bit-for-bit identical, which is what says the
# change reached the hull and nothing else. Anything that moves a cockpit hash
# for a hull change has leaked out of the rasteriser it was meant to be in.
#
# These two are recorded at `--aa`'s default. `--aa 1` measures the outline at
# the subpixel's centre, which is what the rasteriser this replaced did, and it
# reproduces all five of the *then*-committed hashes exactly — that reduction is
# checked in `canvas.rs` against the old code kept there as its oracle, and it
# is the reason this file could be regenerated with any confidence about what
# had changed.
#
# And `warp.txt` moved when the cockpit's tunnel glare was spread out — both
# glows widened by two to three times and dropped to about a quarter of their
# strength, so the light arrives diffused rather than as a hard point inside a
# veil. It was the only hash to move, twice running, and that is the whole
# shape of such a change said back: the two `--demo` flights never leave
# sublight, so the glare block does not run in them at all, and `side.txt` and
# `orbit.txt` go through `add_glow_oval` rather than the cockpit call site. The
# other four were regenerated alongside it both times and were byte-identical.
#
# Those two changes met in a merge, and the file below is what the merged tree
# actually renders rather than one set of hashes pasted beside the other. All
# five were regenerated from it, and each moved exactly where its own change
# said it would: the glare reaches `add_glow` and the hull reaches `fill_hull`,
# and no flight goes through both.
#
# Before all that, `side.txt` moved when the stick was given to the camera out
# here. Only its hint line changed: with the old `QE roll` text restored and
# every other part of that change still in place, all four of the
# then-committed hashes matched, so the camera basis, the star band's vector
# travel, the Doppler axis and the bubble's projected outline are bit-for-bit a
# re-expression of what was there before. The panel names the controls, and the
# controls changed.
#
# The case list lives here, in `tests/golden.rs` and in the `headless` CI job,
# and the file names again in `.gitignore`. Adding a flight means adding it to
# all four.
#
# The hashes hold across build profiles and rustc versions — checked against
# 1.85 and 1.94, debug and release — but not across platforms: sin, cos, exp
# and powf come from the system maths library, and recycling the star pool
# turns a one-ulp difference into a different sky. So this is checked on
# ubuntu-latest only, and a runner image moving to a new glibc may need it
# regenerated once.
